A Bloomwarden in hospice grey stands at the fringe, wringing a poultice she has already wrung dry.
"You came. Good. I need a thing stopped, and I need it stopped the hard way — the kind way."
"Sister Wenlow. I keep the Last-Bloom Hospice. Or I did, until the Greenwatcher started taking my patients."
"It's not killing them. That's the worst part. It loves them. It thinks it's doing them a kindness — getting them into the soil while they're still warm enough to take."
[She finally stops wringing the cloth, and looks at you, frayed all the way through.]
"The Glut taught it mercy, climber. Its own mercy. And now I have to ask you to stop a thing for being exactly as gentle as the rest of us."
"Don't kill it if you can help it. Soothe it. Mend it. Bring it down without bringing it down. I hate this faith. I believe it with my whole heart. Both. Always both."
